Explain abstraction 
Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features.
abstraction can be achieved mainly in 2 ways:
1. Abstract Class
An abstract class can have:
abstract methods (without body)
normal methods (with body)
--
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound(); // abstract method

    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Animal is sleeping");
    }
}
----
class Dog extends Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}
Dog d = new Dog();

d.sound();
d.sleep();

2. Interface
Interface provides 100% abstraction (traditionally).
It contains method declarations.
interface Payment {

    void pay();
}
class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Payment via UPI");
    }
}
Payment p = new UpiPayment();
p.pay();
Ecommerce Payment System
User only does:
pay()
Internal logic:
card validation
bank API
OTP verification
transaction handling
is hidden.
That is abstraction.
Abstraction in Java can be achieved using abstract classes and interfaces.
